---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
import ShotWindow from "../../../../assets/courses/lua-programming/img/2-1-first-window.png";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `skynest/
  main.lua`;

const Figure2 = `function love.load()
  -- runs once, at the start
end

function love.update(dt)
  -- runs every frame, before draw
end

function love.draw()
  -- runs every frame, after update
end`;

const Figure3 = `function love.conf(t)
  t.window.width = 800
  t.window.height = 600
  t.window.title = "Skynest"
end`;

const Task1 = `-- main.lua

function love.draw()
  love.graphics.print("Skynest online", 20, 20)
end`;

const Task2 = `-- main.lua

local frames = 0

function love.load()
  love.graphics.setBackgroundColor(0.05, 0.05, 0.1)
end

function love.update(dt)
  frames = frames + 1
end

function love.draw()
  love.graphics.print("Skynest online", 20, 20)
  love.graphics.print("Frames drawn: " .. frames, 20, 40)
end`;

const Task3Conf = `-- conf.lua

function love.conf(t)
  t.window.width = 800
  t.window.height = 600
  t.window.title = "Skynest"
end`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.1: Your First LÖVE Window</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">LÖVE adds no Lua.</b> Everything in Unit 1
            is everything you need. What LÖVE adds is a window, a clock, a way
            to draw, and a way to read the keyboard &mdash; and it hands those to
            you through ordinary Lua functions you write.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get it from <b>love2d.org</b> and install the version for your
            machine &mdash; on Windows, the <b>64-bit</b> one unless you know
            otherwise. <b class="text-sn-theme">Expect your computer to try to
            stop you.</b> Windows shows a blue "Windows protected your PC" box,
            and macOS says it cannot verify the developer. Neither means
            anything is wrong with the download, but ask your teacher rather
            than clicking through it on a school machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">A game is a folder</h3>

          <p>
            Not a file. LÖVE looks for a folder containing a{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">main.lua</b>, and that is the whole
            requirement.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="none"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            To run it, <b>drag the folder onto the LÖVE application</b>. That
            works everywhere and needs no setup. If you would rather use the
            terminal you can run{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love ."
              lang={"sh"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            from inside the folder, once LÖVE is on your path.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">LÖVE calls you</h3>

          <p>
            This is the one genuinely new idea on the page, and it is worth
            slowing down for. In Unit 1 <i>your</i> file ran from top to bottom
            and finished. Here, <b class="text-sn-theme">LÖVE runs the loop and
            calls your functions</b>. You do not call it, and you never write
            the loop.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <ul>
            <li>
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.load"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              runs <b>once</b>. Set things up here.
            </li>
            <li>
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.update(dt)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              runs <b>every frame</b> &mdash; roughly sixty times a second. All
              your thinking goes here.
            </li>
            <li>
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.draw"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              runs every frame too, straight after update. All your drawing goes
              here, and <b>nothing else</b>.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            You do not have to define all three. A file with only{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.draw"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is a valid game, which is where Task 1 starts.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Notice what these are.</b>{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is a table, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="function love.draw()"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            stores a function in it under the key{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="draw"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />. That is Activity 1.10 and Activity 1.7, and there is no framework
            magic in it &mdash; LÖVE checks whether that key holds a function and
            calls it if so.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Settings live in conf.lua</h3>

          <p>
            An optional second file next to main.lua, read <i>before</i> the
            window is made &mdash; which is why the window size cannot be set
            from{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.load"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="conf.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            LÖVE fills{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="t"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            with every default first and then hands it to you, so you only
            override what you care about. The defaults are <b>800 by 600</b>,
            not resizable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">One thing to expect from Activity 1.4</h3>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">LÖVE runs an older Lua than the one you
            installed for Unit 1.</b> Numbers print without the trailing{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code=".0"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, so{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="10 / 2"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            shows as{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="5"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            here. Two more consequences worth knowing now:{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="//"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            does not exist, so keep using{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="math.floor"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />; and{" "}
            <b>
              <CodeSnippet
                code="math.random"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              gives the identical sequence every run unless you seed it
            </b>, exactly as Activity 1.4 warned.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Where the print output goes</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="print"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            still works and is still your best debugging tool, but it writes to a
            terminal, not the window. Launch from a terminal and you will see it.
            To put text <i>in</i> the window you need{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.graphics.print"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, which is a different function and belongs in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.draw"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Make a folder called <b class="text-sn-theme">skynest</b>. Everything
            in Unit 2 goes in it, and you will keep adding to the same project
            all the way to the end.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: The smallest possible game</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Create <b>main.lua</b> in that folder with nothing but a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.draw"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </li>
            <li>Print some text at 20, 20.</li>
            <li>
              Drag the folder onto LÖVE. <b>A window should open with your text
              in the top-left corner.</b>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Prove the loop is running</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="frames"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              counter as a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="local"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              at the top of the file.
            </li>
            <li>
              Add{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.update"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              and increase it by one each frame.
            </li>
            <li>Draw the count under your text, and add a dark background.</li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">The number should climb fast &mdash;
              about 60 per second.</b> That is the loop, and you never wrote it.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "3-12, 15" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Set up the window</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a <b class="text-sn-theme">conf.lua</b> beside main.lua &mdash;
              a second file, not a change to the first.
            </li>
            <li>Set the width, height, and title.</li>
            <li>
              Run it again. <b>The title bar should change</b>, which is how you
              know LÖVE found the file.
            </li>
            <li>
              Then deliberately misspell{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.conf"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              as{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.config"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              and run once more. <b>No error</b> &mdash; you just silently get
              the defaults, because LÖVE looked up a key that was not there.
              Activity 1.10 explains why that is not a special case.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task3Conf} frame="code" lang="lua" title="conf.lua" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Track elapsed <b>seconds</b> instead of frames by adding{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="dt"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              rather than 1, and print it with{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="string.format"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              to one decimal place. The next two activities are built on the
              difference between those two numbers.
            </li>
            <li>
              Add{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="print(frames)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              inside update, launch from a terminal, and watch it scroll. Then
              take it out again.
            </li>
            <li>
              Set{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="t.window.resizable = true"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              and drag the window edge. Note that your text stays put &mdash;
              Activity 2.2 covers why, and what to do about it.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <img
            src={ShotWindow.src}
            alt="The Skynest window showing two lines of text and a frame counter"
            class="img-fluid rounded d-block mx-auto"
          />
          <p class="text-center text-secondary small mt-2 mb-4">
            Two lines of text and a counter climbing by about sixty a second. That is the whole of Activity 2.1.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no terminal output to compare from here on, so from now on
            these sections show you the window instead. What you should have:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>An <b>800 by 600</b> window titled <b>Skynest</b>.</li>
            <li>A very dark background rather than LÖVE's default.</li>
            <li>Two lines of text in the top-left corner.</li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">A frame count climbing by roughly 60 each
              second.</b>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            If the window opens but is blank, your drawing is probably in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.update"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />. If nothing opens at all, LÖVE could not find{" "}
            <b>main.lua</b> &mdash; check that you dragged the <i>folder</i> and
            not the file.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a class="btn btn-outline-light" href="/courses/lua-programming/">
            Course Index
          </a>
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-primary"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-2/2-2"
          >
            Next: Drawing to the Screen <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
